# ufw: make ports that Docker publishes obey a list, through Docker's DOCKER-USER chain.
#
# Docker sends traffic for a published port through the nat table and the FORWARD chain, so
# ufw's own rules never see it (references/firewall.md has the mechanism). Docker jumps to
# DOCKER-USER first and leaves its content to you. These rules let in only the listed ports
# from the public interface and drop every other new connection to a container from there.
# Containers keep their outbound traffic, and the host and other containers still reach every
# container directly.
#
# Install: append this whole block to the end of /etc/ufw/after.rules (IPv4) and again to the end
# of /etc/ufw/after6.rules (IPv6, which Docker publishes too when a network has IPv6 enabled),
# then reload. Keep a second SSH session open and a timed revert armed while you do it.
#   sudo iptables-restore --test --noflush < block.rules    # syntax check before installing
#   sudo ufw reload
#   sudo iptables -S DOCKER-USER                             # the rules are loaded
# Then test from another machine: the listed port answers, any other published port does not.
#
# The match is on the port the client asked for (--ctorigdstport), which is the published host
# port, not the port inside the container. Add one RETURN line per public port.
#
# Placeholders:
#   <PUBLIC_IF>    the interface that faces the internet, from `ip route show default`
#                  (sample: eth0)
#   <PUBLIC_PORT>  a published host port that should be reachable from the internet
#                  (sample: 443)

*filter
:DOCKER-USER - [0:0]
-A DOCKER-USER -m conntrack --ctstate RELATED,ESTABLISHED -j RETURN
-A DOCKER-USER -i <PUBLIC_IF> -p tcp -m conntrack --ctorigdstport <PUBLIC_PORT> --ctdir ORIGINAL -j RETURN
-A DOCKER-USER -i <PUBLIC_IF> -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -j RETURN
COMMIT
